@Hufe921 , I'd like to explain a bit about my scenario and present an idea I had. The implementation I created allows me to obtain an array with the height of the content of all pages:

```
  public getPagesContentHeight(rowList?: IRow[]) {
    if (!rowList) {
      rowList = this.draw.getRowList()
    }
    const pageRowList: IRow[][] = [[]]
    const internalHeights: number[] = [0]
    const { pageMode } = this.options
    const height = this.draw.getHeight()
    const marginHeight = this.draw.getMainOuterHeight()
    let pageHeight = marginHeight
    let pageNo = 0
    if (pageMode === PageMode.PAGING) {
      for (let i = 0; i < rowList.length; i++) {
        const row = rowList[i]
//        if (row.height) {
          if (
            row.height! + pageHeight > height ||
            rowList[i - 1]?.isPageBreak
          ) {
            pageHeight = marginHeight + row.height
            pageRowList.push([row])
            internalHeights.push(row.height)
            pageNo++
          } else {
            pageHeight += row.height
            pageRowList[pageNo].push(row)
            internalHeights[pageNo] += row.height
          }
//        }
      }
    }
    return internalHeights
  }
```

Another method allows me to pass a set of elements and receive their height:

```
  public getHeightElementList(elementList: IElement[]) {
    let elementHeight = 0
    const margins = this.draw.getMargins()
    const pageHeight = this.draw.getHeight()
    const extraHeight = this.draw.getHeader().getExtraHeight()
    const mainOuterHeight = this.draw.getMainOuterHeight()
    const startX = margins[3]
    const startY = margins[0] + extraHeight
    const surroundElementList = pickSurroundElementList(this.draw.getElementList())
    elementList = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(elementList))
    formatElementList(elementList, {
      isHandleFirstElement: false,
      editorOptions: this.options
    })
    const rowList = this.draw.computeRowList({
      startX,
      startY,
      pageHeight,
      mainOuterHeight,
      isPagingMode: true,
      innerWidth,
      surroundElementList,
      elementList: elementList
    })
    elementHeight = rowList.reduce((accum: number, row) => {
      if (row.height) {
        accum += row.height
      }
      return accum
    }, 0)
    return elementHeight
  }
```

These two methods helped me process the content of each page before inserting it. My scenario works as follows: I create the editor instance initially without data, then load the data from the backend, and then assemble the editor elements piece by piece and insert them.

This way, before inserting into the editor, I get the height of the current page's content (for me, it will always be the last page, as I always insert at the end) with getPagesContentHeight(). Then, with the element ready to be inserted, I get the height of these elements with getHeightElementList(elementList: IElement[]). Then, I get the page height from the editor settings, calculate the remaining page height by taking the difference between the page and the content height of this page, and then check if the height of the elements to be inserted is greater than the remaining height. If so, I add a line break and then insert the elements. Here's a code example:

```
  const height = instance.command.getOptions().height
  const heightContentPage = instance.command.getPagesContentHeight()

  const { dataHeaderProd, dataItensProd, dataBreak } = elem
  const heightDataHeader = instance.command.getHeightElementList([dataHeaderProd])
  const heightDataItens = instance.command.getHeightElementList([dataItensProd])
  const heightDataBreak = instance.command.getHeightElementList([dataBreak])

  const totalHeightProdutos = heightDataHeader + heightDataItens + heightDataBreak

  const remainingHeight = height - heightContentPage[0]
  if (totalHeightProdutos > remainingHeight) {
    elementList.push({ value: '\n', type: ElementType.PAGE_BREAK, width: 774 })
  }
  elementList.push(dataHeaderProd)
  elementList.push(dataItensProd)
  elementList.push(dataBreak)
```

In other words, in my scenario, the process is entirely manual; I process and insert the elements one by one after I have the editor instance. What I was thinking was a way to have the editor do this automatically, but I had difficulty implementing it within Draw. One suggestion I can make is to add a property similar to the CSS break-before, break-inside, and break-after properties. Perhaps simplify a property called break with the values: "before, always-before, after, always-after," or something along those lines. This way, a set of elements could behave similarly to a paragraph, being able to be configured to always break before/after, or be dynamic and break before/after if there's not enough remaining space.

I'd like to know what you think of this idea and what I've already implemented? I tried to think of how to implement the break property, but I couldn't fit it into drarRow(). If you could guide me on this part, or if you think it could be done differently and provide the guidelines, I would be very grateful and excited to implement it.

